<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #eaf2f8;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Introducing Panama to the World – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1 of 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 12th Grade (CEFR: B1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> International Communication</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Introducing Panama to the World</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Follow complex instructions in unfamiliar tasks. Interactive: Collaborate in planning multi-step projects.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can follow complex instructions in planning projects that aim to introduce Panama's culture and heritage to the world (e.g., "First, the traditional dance performers must be scheduled, then the costumes must be prepared, and finally, the cultural showcase must be presented to the international audience.")<br>Can collaborate and ask clarifying questions about task sequences in projects that aim to communicate Panama's national and global importance (e.g., "When should we complete the setup phase?" "It should be done before the morning of the event.")<br>Can interpret roles and tasks from recordings of international planning meetings related to promoting Panama's global relevance (e.g., "The recording mentions that Sara will oversee logistics for the cultural exhibit, while Ana and Ivan will manage the trade presentation on the Panama Canal")</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #eaf2f8;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 55%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 10%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing a short, engaging video clip (1-2 minutes) without sound, showcasing iconic Panamanian scenes (Panama Canal, biodiversity, Indigenous cultures, modern city skyline). Students listen to the teacher's instructions: "Watch this video about Panama. What do you think are the most important aspects Panama wants to *introduce* to the *global* community?" Students briefly discuss in pairs.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces the lesson's key vocabulary by writing the words on the board: <b>global, trade, relevance, hub, canal, recognize, impact, proficient, strategic, multinational</b>. The teacher models the pronunciation of these words, emphasizing /t/ in "trade" and /g/ in "global". "Listen carefully: *global*, *trade*. Repeat after me. We will also learn how to stress question words and modals like 'must' and 'should' for clarity in communication." The teacher then uses sentences to demonstrate: "Panama *is recognized* for its *strategic* location as a *global trade hub*." "Students *must be proficient* in English."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the key vocabulary and concepts through direct questions and examples. For example:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If something is 'global', does it affect only one country or many countries?" (Expected answer: "Many countries.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does it mean if Panama is a 'trade hub'? Is it a central point for business or a small local market?" (Expected answer: "A central point for business.")</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "International Communication")</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students listen to an authentic audio recording (approx. 2-3 minutes) of an excerpt from an "International Planning Meeting" where representatives discuss strategies for an upcoming global expo focused on "Introducing Panama to the World." The recording features dialogue about project phases, roles, and deadlines. The register is professional and formal. The audio includes instances of passive voice ("Panama *is recognized*..."), modals of necessity ("We *must ensure*..."), and relative clauses ("...the Panama Canal, *which connects*...").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Can interpret roles and tasks from recordings of international planning meetings related to promoting Panama's global relevance.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the audio twice.</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First listening (Gist):</b> Students listen for the main idea. "What is the primary purpose of this meeting?" (e.g., "To plan for a global expo about Panama.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second listening (True/False & Information Matching):</b> Students listen again and complete a True/False exercise based on specific statements about the meeting's agenda (e.g., "The cultural exhibit will be managed by Ivan. True/False"). They also match speakers to responsibilities mentioned in the recording using a simple worksheet (e.g., Speaker A -> Logistics, Speaker B -> Marketing).</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Focusing on listening for specific details, identifying passive voice, modals of necessity, and relative clauses. Target vocabulary: <b>global, trade, relevance, hub, canal, recognize, impact, proficient, strategic, multinational</b>. Pronunciation focus: Stress on Wh- question words (e.g., "WHAT MAKES PANama...") and modal verbs (e.g., "You MUST be proficient...").</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Gap-fill based on listening):</b> Students receive a transcript of segments from the audio with gaps for key vocabulary, passive voice structures, modals, or relative clauses. They listen to these specific segments again and fill in the blanks. Example: "Panama ____ for its *trade influence*." (is recognized). "We ____ be *proficient* in English to present effectively." (must).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Sentence Completion & Rephrasing):</b> The teacher plays sentences from the audio, stopping before a key phrase. Students listen and complete the sentence using the target grammar or vocabulary. For instance, "The recording emphasized that Panama is a *strategic*..." (hub). Then, the teacher provides sentence starters and asks students to rephrase information from the recording using passive voice or relative clauses, focusing on listening for information to complete their sentences. Example: Teacher: "Panama connects continents." Student (listening for information and rephrasing): "Panama is a hub *that connects continents*."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Peer Check & Clarification):</b> In pairs, students compare their completed gap-fills and rephrased sentences. They take turns reading their answers aloud, actively listening to their partner for accuracy and natural pronunciation of the target words and grammatical structures. They are encouraged to ask clarifying questions: "Did you hear 'multinational' or 'national'?" "When should we complete the setup phase?" (Practicing pragmatic language and stress on Wh- questions).</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "International Communication")</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Collaborative Project Planning (Simulation of an International Meeting).</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives a brief written scenario: "Your team has been tasked with organizing a 'Panama Global Showcase' event to be presented to an international audience. You need to plan the sequence of tasks." The teacher then plays a new audio recording (approx. 1.5 minutes) which provides complex instructions for planning the event, including roles, deadlines, and a multi-step project sequence (e.g., "First, the traditional dance performers *must be scheduled*, then the costumes *must be prepared*, and finally, the cultural showcase *must be presented* to the international audience"). The recording also mentions that "the trade presentation *is managed* by a separate team, but coordination *is required*."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students listen carefully to the instructions. They then collaborate to create a concise action plan for their showcase, orally discussing and outlining the steps, roles, and materials needed.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They *must* use the target vocabulary (e.g., *global, strategic, impact, recognize, multinational*) and grammar (passive voice, modals of necessity, relative clauses) as they discuss.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The focus is on active listening to each other's contributions and the detailed instructions from the recording, asking clarifying questions ("WHAT *must be scheduled* first?", "When *should* the costumes be ready?"), and collaboratively structuring their plan.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group appoints a "lead presenter" who will briefly present their team's plan to the class.</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy: Teacher Observation Checklist & Peer Assessment.</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist to note how well groups follow complex instructions, collaborate, and use target language. Students also use a simple peer assessment checklist to evaluate their group members' listening and participation.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task: Group Presentation of Action Plan.</b> Each group's "lead presenter" briefly (1-2 minutes) presents their team's action plan for the "Panama Global Showcase." The rest of the class listens actively and prepares one clarifying question to ask the presenting group, practicing "listening for specific information" and "asking relevant questions."</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Comprehension:</b> Ability to accurately follow multi-step instructions from the audio recording.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Active participation and listening during group planning.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Integration of at least 3-4 key vocabulary words (e.g., *global, strategic, impact, hub*).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Grammar:</b> Appropriate use of passive voice and modals of necessity.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity & Intelligibility:</b> Overall comprehensibility and pronunciation, especially of /t/, /g/, and stressed Wh- questions/modals.</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"List three key instructions you listened for during the planning meeting."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new word or phrase related to 'International Communication' that you learned today?"</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7-8 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What listening strategies did you use today that helped you understand the complex instructions?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel about discussing Panama's global relevance after today's lesson?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well today? (e.g., Students effectively collaborated, vocabulary pronunciation was accurate, most students understood the main gist.)</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments and scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., Perhaps more explicit practice on identifying passive voice in audio, or provide a pre-listening vocabulary matching task for less proficient students).</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Today we focused on listening to understand how to introduce Panama to the world. In Lesson 2, we will *read* an article that uses many of these same vocabulary words and ideas to deepen our comprehension of Panama's international standing. This will prepare us for discussing and presenting these ideas in future lessons."</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
    </tr>
</table>